It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-06 16:48:34 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-06 23:48:34 +0700\n\nCHANGES\n\n1. **Renewal advances tenure before carriage is proved.** In `driver.cljc:718–732`, `renew` passes the front-inbound append result to `lease/observe-renewal`. That only proves the request was queued: `authority/front.cljc:211–226` can subsequently answer `:suspended` without carrying the renewal. Retain the request and its pre-send clock reading; advance tenure only on authenticated, matching `:carried` evidence. Recheck the existing bound before accepting delayed evidence; a late acknowledgment must never revive expired tenure. Pin inbound acceptance followed by failed carriage, delayed acknowledgment, and successful carriage.\n\n2. **Proposal and release progress can be lost.** `control-reply` (`driver.cljc:630–643`) marks carriage without checking `:yin.k/answer`; `step-releasing` (`873–887`) declares release complete on inbound acceptance and stops reading replies. Proposals likewise stop resending once queued. Retain these obligations until authenticated carriage, retry unchanged requests after suspended carriage or lost replies, and distinguish lease-proposal rejection from transport/carriage failure. Pin a full lease-fact stream behind an accepting inbound stream: proposals must eventually progress and releases must eventually be carried, without clearing quarantine or manufacturing completion.\n\n3. **The tenure check does not cover subsequent IO.** `step-active` checks once (`driver.cljc:840–847`), then `run-cycle` executes `vm/run`, writer emission and live observation (`778–788`) without another check. Computation or an earlier effect can consume the remaining tenure. Enforce the D10 ruling’s recheck before subsequent program IO, including bare writes and closes; checking only the request callback misses those direct effects. Pin expiry during computation and between successive effects: no further program IO, root and children ended, release retained for cleanup.\n\n4. **Thrown post-grant failures bypass cleanup.** `run-cycle` and `step-active` have no failure transition around execution/emission. The landed writer explicitly throws for terminal bare-link append outcomes (`holder/writer.cljc:278`), so this is a reachable path, not hypothetical seam misuse. Preserve the failure while transitioning into an ended state with a retained release obligation; do not retry the failed program effect. Pin that terminal-link path and a release initially unable to be carried.\n\nThe admitted-variant proof and composition of the existing lower, writer and reader are appropriate, but these defects violate the remote-carriage, tenure and post-grant cleanup contracts. The current tests carry the front immediately after driver steps, which masks the distinction between inbound acceptance and actual carriage. These are D13 obligations, not D14 journal work. No files were edited and no suites were run."}}
